{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Operator-approved scope\nImplement the finalized 2026-09-02 scope from the ticket comments: rebar LLM gate operations must emit lightweight keepalive lines synchronously at existing progress seams so supervising stall watchdogs are not silent for an entire multi-call gate phase.\n\n## Implementation plan\n1. Add a small shared LLM keepalive helper with a module-level interval constant (~20-30 seconds) and a single last-emit-timestamp comparison. It logs at WARNING so the line reaches stderr at rebar's default effective log level (WARNING) without REBAR_LOG_LEVEL.\n2. Emit through that helper at the primary per-LLM-call seam in `src/rebar/llm/runner.py`, including operation/phase name, call index when available, and elapsed seconds only. No prompt, response, tool args, or tool payload content may be logged.\n3. Emit through the same helper at tool-call boundaries in `src/rebar/llm/agent_call.py` and workflow-step boundaries in `src/rebar/llm/workflow/executor.py`; the shared elapsed-time coalescing must prevent bursts of fast tool/step calls from producing one line per call.\n4. Keep the change purely additive: no background daemon thread, no progress-gating/stall escalation/health withdrawal, no total-runtime cap, and no change to existing timeout behavior, preserving ADR 0089.\n5. Add RED-first deterministic unit coverage with fake clock/log capture showing (a) a gate/agentic-loop invocation emits keepalive(s) at the default log level within a bounded window, and (b) rapid tool-call bursts are coalesced. Validate the existing silence-tolerance tests remain green: `tests/unit/test_llm_timeouts.py::test_no_total_runtime_timer_mechanism` and `::test_slow_but_alive_server_completes_under_read_timeout`.\n6. Add a short warning to gate documentation that gate ops can exceed common 600s supervisor stall budgets while silent between calls; the new keepalive mitigates multi-call silence, but a single very long request can still exceed a budget. Note `DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_S=600` is per request and close can make many requests, so supervisors may need larger no-output budgets.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] A gate/agentic-loop path that crosses per-call progress emits at least one keepalive line to stderr at the default log level, driven by real per-call progress and well inside a 600s watchdog budget.\n- [ ] Bursts of rapid tool calls/steps do not produce one line per call; keepalives are coalesced by elapsed time.\n- [ ] Keepalive content is limited to phase/op name, call index where available, and elapsed seconds; no prompt/response/tool payload content is logged.\n- [ ] No timer thread, progress-gating/stall escalation, total-runtime cap, or timeout change is introduced; ADR 0089 silence-tolerance tests stay green.\n- [ ] Gate docs contain the rescoped supervisor-budget warning.\n\n## Portability\nThe implementation is pure Python logging plus deterministic unit tests. It does not depend on any CI provider, external daemon, terminal feature, or platform-specific supervisor, satisfying project portability.","ticket_type":"task"}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788382943777025489,"uuid":"09e386e2-f161-474a-ac5e-7be82662e313"}